'''Prehistoric Northwestern Kaftia''' spans the period from the earliest evidence of human habitation in the [[Pfein Sea]] basin (approximately 200,000 years ago) to the appearance of iron working around 1,000 BCE. The era covers the entry of humans into the basin from central [[Kaftia]], the long marine occupation of the crater shore, the division of the founding population into shore and highland branches, the pluvial green phase and its end in the desiccation of central Kaftia, the domestication of wheat in the [[Bande River]] valley, and the emergence of the first farming and trading communities. Beyond the basin, the wider region held mobile hunter-gatherer and nomadic populations, linked to the basin by trade across the Yaweka crescent with the [[Sankani Sea]] coast to the north.

== Environmental setting ==

The [[Pfein Sea]] fills the impact crater formed by the meteor that ended the Cretaceous period, excavated in what was probably a peninsula of northwestern Kaftia. The [[Yaweka Mountain Range]], a crescent of eroded rim uplift, forms the eastern and northern land borders of the sea, and the broader basin around it is the [[Northwest Kaftian Depression]]. The range is not high — it is an uplift byproduct of the impact — but it forms a natural division between the inner-ring states that cover the crater and the outer-ring states beyond it. The Pfein Sea is marine: it connects to the [[Ethiopian Ocean]] through the [[Strait of Sindewand]] at its southern end, and its waters never desiccated. It covers approximately 285,753 km², with a central basin reaching 800–1,200 m in depth and shallow terraced margins.

The shores of the sea carry a Mediterranean climate with winter rains. East of the Yaweka crescent the country is drier, a belt of semi-arid steppe. The impact scattered glass across the surrounding plains, and Pfein impact glass served as a raw material for tools from the earliest occupation onward.

Because the sea never dried, the basin retained water, fish, and game through the arid phases of the Pleistocene, while the plains beyond the crescent oscillated between steppe and desert. Archaeologists describe the basin as a refugium: through each arid phase, shore populations persisted while the plains were abandoned, and each pluvial phase brought renewed expansion outward. The shore terraces of the sea accordingly preserve a nearly unbroken sequence of occupation layers spanning roughly 200,000 years.

== Paleolithic ==

=== Arrival from central Kaftia ===
The earliest archaeological evidence of humans in the basin dates to approximately 200,000 BP. The first groups entered overland from central Kaftia to the south, during a pluvial phase in which steppe corridors opened across the belt that now forms the deserts of central Kaftia. The oldest sites are campsites and lithic scatters on the raised shore terraces of the eastern Pfein coast, of which the type site is Sangwe Bluff.

The '''Sangwe tradition''' (~200,000–40,000 BP), named for that site, is a {{W|prepared-core technique|prepared-core}} lithic tradition. Its makers worked local stone and Pfein impact glass into cutting tools, and the continuity of the tradition's core-reduction methods across its long span is one of the principal arguments for the continuous occupation of the basin.

=== Shore adaptation ===
From an early date the basin population subsisted from the marine shore: fish, shellfish, sea turtles, and waterfowl. {{W|Shell midden|Shell middens}} accumulated along the shore terraces, following the fluctuations of the sea level. The shore economy was marine, distinct from the freshwater fishing traditions that developed around the [[Great Kaftian Lake]] to the south.

=== Later Stone Age ===
[[File:Yaweka_Rock_Art.png|thumb|right|250px|Rock art panel from the Ndewe Caves depicting a shore fishing scene (~20,000 BP)]]
<!-- Image prompt: prehistoric rock art panel in red and ochre pigment on a cave wall, depicting human figures with reed boats, harpoons, and large fish along a shoreline, in the style of ancient cave painting -->
The '''Ndewe industry''' (~40,000–10,000 BP), named for the Ndewe Caves in the Yaweka foothills, marks the Later Stone Age of the basin. It is characterised by {{W|microlith}} technology and composite tools, and its sites include the first stable shore settlements: permanent camps whose midden deposits record a settled fishing economy. Reed watercraft appear in the record of this period.

The caves and overhangs of the Yaweka crescent hold a long sequence of {{W|rock art}}. The earliest panels depict hunters and the megafauna of the pluvial plains; later panels show fishing scenes, boats, and cattle; the latest show cultivated fields and settlements. The sequence serves as a chronological index for the basin's prehistory.

== Green phase (c. 12,000–6,000 BP) ==

Between approximately 12,000 and 6,000 BP a pluvial phase covered the plains east of the crescent in grassland, seasonal rivers, and marshes. The basin's population expanded inland from the shore, and pottery appears in the record for the first time.

Herding and cultivation arrived at the end of the phase from the south. {{W|Zebu}} cattle, domesticated c. 5000 BCE in the [[Dakare-Galwa Wetlands]] of southern Kaftia, spread north through the still-open corridor together with cultivated {{W|sorghum}} and {{W|pearl millet}}. These arrivals were the basin's first food-producing economy, and they passed through the corridor in its final centuries of viability.

== Desiccation of central Kaftia (c. 6,000–4,000 BP) ==

Between approximately 6,000 and 4,000 BP the rains withdrew. The plains reverted to steppe and then to desert, and the belt between the basin and southern Kaftia became the desert that now covers central Kaftia, including the country of [[Tamazgha]]. Overland trade with southern Kaftia was greatly reduced.

The basin's population contracted to the shore, the [[Bande River]] valley, and the springs of the Yaweka crescent. The economy intensified around fishing, herding, and simple irrigation off seasonal rivers. Long-distance exchange reoriented toward the sea: coastal routes around the Pfein Sea, and through the [[Strait of Sindewand]] to the [[Ethiopian Ocean]].

== Food production (c. 5,000–3,000 BP) ==

[[File:Mbandezi_Excavation.png|thumb|left|250px|Excavated Mbandezi-culture village site on the Bande River (~2,000 BCE)]]
<!-- Image prompt: archaeological excavation of a prehistoric river-valley village, showing circular stone-footing house foundations, storage pits with charred grain, and the Bande River in the background with arid hills beyond, dry Mediterranean setting -->
The '''Mbandezi culture''' (~3000–1000 BCE), named for its type site on the [[Bande River]] along the modern Bonande–Yaweka border, is the food-producing complex of the basin. Its villages combined wheat cultivation, sorghum and pearl millet from the southern package, zebu herding, and shore fishing.

{{W|Wheat}} was domesticated in the Bande valley from local wild grasses. The domestication was independent of the wheat domestication of the eastern [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] region, and wheat is the crop for which Adonian archaeobotany recognises two independent domestication events. {{W|Barley}} reached the basin through later trade routes. The Mbandezi culture's trade goods included Pfein impact glass, salt from evaporite pans along the shore, and dried fish.

== Iron Age transition (after c. 1,000 BCE) ==

Iron working reached the basin around 1,000 BCE, closing the prehistoric period. Iron tools and weapons spread along the shore and river corridors, village societies expanded, and the polities of the [[Ancient Bonande|ancient period]] developed from the shore and river-valley communities of the Mbandezi culture.

== Beyond the Pfein basin ==

Outside the Pfein basin, the prehistoric record of northwestern Kaftia is sparse. The ridge of the Yaweka crescent divides the inner-ring states that cover the crater — [[Sindewand]], [[Zetaba]], [[Wolffrea]], [[Yaweka]], and [[Bonande]] — from the outer ring beyond it: [[Alwana]], [[Tategazi]], and [[Makombu]] on the coast of the [[Sankani Sea]], and [[Senara]] inland between Makombu and Bonande. The outer country held no permanent settlements of comparable scale to the basin's shore and river-valley villages; its populations were hunter-gatherer and nomadic groups whose mobile camps left light archaeological traces. From the green phase onward, some of these groups adopted zebu herding and became pastoral nomads.

No desert divides the north. Unlike the southern corridor, which the desiccation of central Kaftia closed, the route over the low northern arm of the Yaweka crescent remained open through every arid phase, and exchange between the two coasts was probably continuous through the whole period.

The Sankani Sea, also known as the Sankani Gulf, is an arm of the [[Ethiopian Ocean]] north of the crescent. Shells originating from the Sankani have been recovered from excavation sites in the Pfein basin, and Pfein impact glass appears at sites on the Sankani coast. The two finds indicate a two-way trade across the crescent, carried by the mobile groups of the country between the coasts.

== Peoples and languages ==

The prehistoric population of the basin descends from the single founding population that entered approximately 200,000 BP. Over the course of the Stone Age it divided into two branches: the shore and river-valley groups, ancestral to the [[Bande peoples]], and the highland groups of the Yaweka crescent, ancestral to the [[Yaweka peoples]]. The divergence of the two branches is not precisely dated.

The two branches of the [[Bonandei–Yawekai languages|Bonandei–Yawekai language family]] correspond to this population division: [[Bonandei language|Bonandei]] on the shore and rivers, [[Yawekai language|Yawekai]] in the highlands.

The hunter-gatherer and nomadic groups of the wider region were probably related to the basin's founding population, but the archaeological and linguistic record does not confirm the relationship.

The modern [[Bonandian people|Bonandian]] identity did not exist in prehistory. The name arose around 1100 CE, when the shore and river-valley groups coalesced; the collective form preserves the name of the Bande River and of the pre-coalescence groups. The Yaweka remained outside the coalescence and retain their own claim of descent from the founding population.

== Genetic history ==

Genetic studies of modern Bonandian and Yaweka populations show a closely related pair of clusters with deep common ancestry and high continuity with the basin's founding population, in keeping with the single-entry settlement of the basin and its long isolation. Both clusters carry a minor contribution from southern Kaftian lineages, attributed to the green-phase contacts with the Dakare-Galwa region. Admixture from later outside populations is limited, a pattern attributed to the desert belt that has surrounded the basin since the desiccation.

